\title{What a theorem about an extracted kernel does not say about the C++ code}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{extraction}
\tag{trust}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open.}
\p{Since [dec-03IY](dec-03IY) an extracted function is a total Lean function, and \code{\verb~|CorrectlyRounded~} is about its results. Three things that the earlier semantics of the IR stated, or that a reader may assume, are outside the statements. They were not listed anywhere before the review of [ses-L0FW](ses-L0FW).}
\ul{
\li{\strong{Undefined behaviour.} \code{\verb~|Core.fptosi~} and \code{\verb~|Core.fptoui~} return \code{\verb~|0~} where the conversion is out of range, a NaN or an infinity; C++ gives these no meaning. A table is read with \code{\verb~|[i]!~}, which returns a default when the index is out of bounds. The earlier semantics had executions that go wrong and required that there be none ([log-VH6E](log-VH6E)). To state it again, give these operations a result in \code{\verb~|Option~}, or prove for each kernel, on the ranges where it is used, that the argument is in range.}
\li{\strong{Effects.} \code{\verb~|ast2lean --drop-effects~} removes \code{\verb~|errno~} and the floating-point exception flags. \code{\verb~|Core.setOverflow~} and \code{\verb~|Core.setUnderflow~} return the value only.}
\li{\strong{The translation.} \code{\verb~|ast2lean~}, about 5 700 lines of C++, and \code{\verb~|LibcMath/Core/~} are trusted. The evidence is the differential test: on 2026-11-24, each of the five compiled plans agreed with its kernel on 10 199 702 inputs in each rounding direction it supports. For binary32 the test can cover all \code{\verb~|2³²~} inputs in each direction, at about the cost of one compiled exhaustive check; that was not run.}
\li{\strong{NaNs.} A kernel returns the NaN that FloatLib computes. The statements compare results up to the identity of NaNs, so this matters only where code reads the bits of a NaN that an operation produced.}
}
\p{\strong{Plan, 2026-11-24.} The maintainer asked for the statement to be restored. The extractor's intermediate form is an expression tree, so a second pass over it can emit, next to each function \code{\verb~|f~}, a Boolean \code{\verb~|f.defined~} with the same parameters: the conjunction, along the path that is taken, of the conditions under which each operation has a meaning in C++ (a conversion to an integer in range, a table index in bounds, a shift by less than the width, no signed overflow, a divisor that is not zero). \code{\verb~|CorrectAt~} then asks for \code{\verb~|defined~} as well as for the value; the short-path proofs show it where no such operation is reached, and a check of a block tests it with the value. The values of the kernels, and the proofs about them, stay as they are.}
